From e734dc1b08179b839ad5414e5bf8c4ae6d2560ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dist <build@localhost>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 13:59:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: add static USDT trace points on the syscall, unix-call
 and wineserver paths

Result

The interface profiler needs every crossing between Live's PE code and
the unix side of Wine, with the caller and the result, without a patch
per syscall or unix call. ntdll.so now carries eight static user-space
trace points (SystemTap SDT notes, provider "wine") on the two x86_64
dispatchers and on the wineserver request and wait paths. An eBPF tracer
attaches to them by file and pid. Unattached, a site is one 10-byte nop
(nopw %cs:0x0(%rax,%rax,1)) and its description lives in the
non-allocated .note.stapsdt section: no branch, no load, no runtime
state and no semaphore, so the cost is zero. Attached, the kernel puts a
uprobe on the nop. The 10-byte form is the only one Linux 7.2 optimizes:
the first hit rewrites the site to "lea -0x80(%rsp),%rsp; call" into
the process's uprobe trampoline, and every later hit enters the kernel
through a syscall instead of an int3 trap, about 170 instead of 430 ns
per hit on the profiling laptop. The form is also a requirement: a
kernel that does not optimize this nop (Linux before 7.2) refuses a
uprobe on it, because its %cs prefix makes uprobe_init_insn() return
-ENOTSUPP (error 524), so tracing these sites needs Linux 7.2 or later.
The earlier 5-byte nop could be traced on any kernel, through int3.

Scope

dlls/ntdll/unix/sdt.h is systemtap's <sys/sdt.h> (CC0) with the probe
site changed on x86_64 (_SDT_DEF_NOP) to that 10-byte nop, emitted as
.byte through a new ten-argument _SDT_ASM_10 and aligned to 16 bytes so
that no site can cross a 4 KiB page (Linux 7.2.9 puts no uprobe at all
on a 10-byte nop that does), and with one line of trailing whitespace
blanked; its provenance block lists the changes. sdt-config.h
replaces the autoconf-generated file and enables the "?" and "R" section
flags (binutils 2.36 and later). Both headers are private to the ntdll
unix side; dlls/ntdll/Makefile.in lists no headers and is unchanged.
sync.c is untouched, so performance/0001 keeps applying. A pre-C99 -std
stops signal_x86_64.c with one #error, because its raw templates need
the variadic STAP_PROBE_ASM of sdt.h.

Probes in the dispatchers (signal_x86_64.c), as asm templates with
decimal displacements, no commas and a single % (basic asm):

  wine:syscall_entry(id, frame, user_rsp, user_rip)
    -4@176(%rcx) 8@%rcx 8@136(%rcx) 8@112(%rcx)
    After the kernel-stack switch and the FS setup, before the table
    lookup. frame->syscall_id (bits 12-13 select the table, so 0x1000
    and up is win32u), the frame, frame->rsp (the return address into
    the NtXxx caller is at *user_rsp) and frame->rip (NtXxx+0x14). %rcx
    holds the frame on every Linux sub-path: the arch_prctl path
    restores it from %rbp before the common code.
  wine:syscall_exit(status, frame, id, restore_flags)
    -4@%eax 8@%rcx -4@176(%rcx) -4@180(%rcx)
    At the common __wine_syscall_dispatcher_return label, after its CFI
    directives and before the stack move. Every jump to the label sets
    %rcx to the frame first. 0x20000 in restore_flags marks the exit of
    a unix call whose frame was modified. Thread start enters the label
    once without an entry; a tracer discounts that exit per thread.
  wine:unixcall_entry(handle, code, frame, caller_rip)
    8@%r10 -4@%edx 8@%rsp 8@112(%rsp)
    Before "callq *(%r10,%rdx,8)". %r10 is the module's unixlib table,
    %edx the function code, %rsp the frame (the arch_prctl path clobbers
    %rcx here) and frame->rip the PE caller, because __wine_unix_call is
    an inline function.
  wine:unixcall_exit(status, frame)
    -4@%eax 8@%rcx
    After the call's "movq %rsp,%rcx".

An optimized hit stores 32 bytes just below the red zone, from %rsp -
160 to %rsp - 129 (the return address and the trampoline's saved %rcx,
%r11 and %rax), and gives back %rsp, the flags and every register.
Every dispatcher site runs on the kernel stack with %rsp at or below the
syscall frame and only unused stack beneath it, so those stores cannot
clobber live data; at the C sites the ABI leaves the stack below the
red zone free.

Probes in C (server.c), through STAP_PROBE1 and STAP_PROBE2:

  wine:server_req_start(req_type) and
  wine:server_req_end(req_type, status) in server_call_unlocked, around
    send_request and wait_reply. The request type is read before
    send_request because the reply overwrites the request union.
  wine:server_wait_start(flags) and wine:server_wait_end(status) in
    server_select, around wait_select_reply, where waits that do not
    use NTSync and alertable waits block.

server.c also carries the literal WINE_USDT_V2 for the build audit; V1
marked the earlier 5-byte nop sites, which the kernel does not optimize.

Verification

On the CachyOS profiling laptop (Linux 7.2.9, gcc 16.2, binutils 2.47)
the series applies to the vendored base with git am --3way, followed by
performance/0001. A test program built against the patched sdt.h, with
a STAP_PROBE3 site, a STAP_PROBEV site and three STAP_PROBE_ASM sites in
a global asm function that use the dispatchers' argument strings, has
the 10-byte nop on a 16-byte boundary at every site; readelf -n shows
the notes with those argument strings and bpftrace -l lists the probes.
With bpftrace attached, every site read back from /proc/<pid>/mem as
48 8d 64 24 80 e8 and a call into the [uprobes-trampoline] mapping, all
arguments arrived intact, the %rsp-relative ones included, the
registers, the carry flag and the red zone survived every hit, and a
counting probe cost about 160 to 200 ns per hit against about 420 to
460 ns for the 5-byte nop. A 10-byte nop placed across a page boundary
got no breakpoint: with bpftrace attached first it never fired in 1000
calls, and attaching to the running process failed with error 524
(ENOTSUPP), while one ending on the last byte of a page was optimized
and a 5-byte nop across a page still trapped. The two dispatchers of
the patched signal_x86_64.c, assembled on their own with wine/asm.h and
dwarf.h, have the 10-byte nop on a 16-byte boundary at all four sites.
Linked with copies of the server.c probes into a shared object, all
eight notes and the WINE_USDT_V2 literal survive strip --strip-unneeded
and the build audit's --check-usdt-sites passes every site;
.note.stapsdt is not allocated and .stapsdt.base carries the AGR flags
in the object file.
